    "overview": "Financial exchanges disseminate market updates (bids, asks, trades) via UDP Multicast protocols like ITCH and accept orders via low-overhead binary TCP protocols like OUCH. Because UDP is unreliable, exchanges broadcast two identical redundant feeds over physically separate network paths (Feed A and Feed B). Feed handlers employ real-time packet arbitration to eliminate packet drop latency without waiting for TCP retransmissions.",
    "theorySections": [
      {
        "heading": "1. Binary Exchange Protocols: Why JSON and XML Are Banned",
        "content": "Human-readable formats (JSON, XML, FIX string) require string tokenization, delimiter scanning, and decimal conversions that waste hundreds of CPU cycles.\nModern exchange protocols use fixed-offset binary structs (e.g. ITCH 5.0):\n```c\n#pragma pack(push, 1)\nstruct ItchAddOrderMsg {\n    char     msg_type;        // 'A' (1 byte)\n    uint16_t stock_locate;    // Stock ID (2 bytes)\n    uint16_t tracking_number; // Tracking (2 bytes)\n    uint64_t timestamp_ns;    // Nanoseconds since midnight (6 bytes / 48-bit)\n    uint64_t order_ref_num;   // Unique Order ID (8 bytes)\n    char     buy_sell_ind;    // 'B' or 'S' (1 byte)\n    uint32_t shares;          // Share quantity (4 bytes)\n    char     stock[8];        // Ticker symbol ASCII (8 bytes)\n    uint32_t price;           // Fixed-point price (4 decimal places) (4 bytes)\n};\n#pragma pack(pop)\n```\nParsing requires ZERO string operations—simply cast the packet pointer to the struct!"
      },
      {
        "heading": "2. Real-Time A/B Feed Arbitration Architecture",
        "content": "Exchanges publish identical MoldUDP64 packet streams over Line A and Line B.\nThe feed handler maintains the expected next sequence number:\n1. Packet arrives from Line A with seq=1001. Handler processes it and updates `expected_seq = 1002`.\n2. Duplicate packet arrives from Line B with seq=1001. Handler immediately drops it in 3 nanoseconds!\n3. If Line A drops packet seq=1002 but Line B delivers seq=1002, Line B seamlessly bridges the gap with ZERO packet loss and ZERO wait time.\nOnly when BOTH Line A and Line B drop a sequence does the feed handler trigger a MoldUDP64 TCP gap-fill request."
      }
    ],
